Expanding Oracle Indexes: Adding Columns Can you add a column to an existing index in Oracle?
Introduction When working with large databases, it&rsquo;s common to have indexes to improve query performance. Although, sometimes you may need to make changes to the index, such as adding a new column. In this post, we will answer the question, &ldquo;Can you add a column to an existing index in Oracle?&rdquo;
The Answer The short answer is no.

Get String After Character in Oracle: A Quick Guide in the summary, use more descriptive words like &ldquo;extracting a substring after a specific character.&rdquo;
Summary: In Oracle SQL, we can use various functions to extract a substring after a specific character from a given string. We can use the SUBSTR() and INSTR() functions or the REGEXP_SUBSTR() function to achieve this.
Using SUBSTR() and INSTR(): We can use the SUBSTR() and INSTR() functions to extract the substring after the hyphen (&quot;-&quot;) character.

ORACLE: Fixing &lsquo;babel-register&rsquo; Module Not Found Error How to Fix &ldquo;Cannot Find Module &lsquo;babel-register&rsquo; Error&rdquo;
If you are working on a project that involves making an oracle connecting to an Express back-end and you encounter the error &ldquo;Cannot find module &lsquo;babel-register&rsquo;,&rdquo; don&rsquo;t worry. There are a few possible reasons for this error, and we&rsquo;ll explore some of them in this Q&amp;A Session.
Before we dive into the solutions, let&rsquo;s first know what the error message means.

Switching from Oracle to MySQL Introduction: Migrating a large-scale Oracle database to MySQL can be a challenging task, especially when trying to ensure that the data is consistent and minimising any downtime. In this blog post, we will tackle some of the common questions and challenges that arise in such migration scenarios.
Tagline: &ldquo;Migrate from Oracle to MySQL without the headache: Deep Dive into Best Practices and Tools.&rdquo;
What is the best way to migrate an Oracle database to MySQL?

ORACLE: Oracle SQL&rsquo;s Equivalent of SHOW CREATE TABLE Oracle SQL equivalent for &ldquo;show create table&rdquo;
When working with MySQL, developers often use the &ldquo;show create table&rdquo; command to view the definition of a table, including columns and their data types. In Oracle SQL, there are a couple of options for viewing similar information about a table.
The DESC Command The DESC command in Oracle SQL displays the structure of a table, including column names and data types, but does not provide additional details such as constraints or indexes.
